## Phase 1 - Harden the reusable common, syntax, and build tools

☑ Audit `NewTools/Common.ps1`, `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1`, and `Build.ps1` for
  sibling-project names, paths, IDs, commands, and assumptions; remove every
  project-specific leak.
☑ Keep the new build defaults intact, especially `LiveReload=$true`,
  `.env.local`, `src/info.txt`, and `<mod-id> reload`.
☑ Reconcile `Build.ps1 -SourceDir` with syntax checking so a caller cannot
  select one source tree for metadata while `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1` silently
  validates another. Either pass explicit search roots into the syntax checker
  or remove any parameter whose advertised behavior is not real.
☑ Make syntax-check output distinguish production files from test/support
  files and report accurate counts when `-IncludeTests` is active.
☑ Verify explicit and environment-based compiler resolution, including the
  configured Lua-version mismatch failure.
☑ Verify `.env.local` parsing for comments, optional `export`, quoted values,
  missing optional files, invalid entries, and precedence of process variables
  over file values.
☑ Verify DFHack runner resolution through an explicit path, a Dwarf Fortress
  root, and PATH, with clear failures for missing runner files.
☑ Verify reload output is printed, optionally captured to
  `ReloadOutputPath`, and preserved in the failure report when reload exits
  nonzero.
☑ Add `.env.local` to `.gitignore`; add a safe example/template only if the
  required variables cannot be documented clearly without one.

Exit gate: the reusable build stack validates the intended files, retains all
accepted defaults, contains no sibling-project leakage, and has focused proof
for configuration and failure behavior.

### Phase 1 recorded evidence (2026-07-17)

Implementation changes in staged `NewTools/`:

- `Check-LuaSyntax.ps1` now accepts `SourceDir` and `TestsDir`, resolves an
  explicit production directory, and reports separate production and
  test/support file counts. `Build.ps1` resolves and forwards its `SourceDir`,
  so validation and live metadata now use the same source tree.
- Empty test directories remain a real empty array under StrictMode instead of
  becoming `$null` and failing on `.Count`.
- `Build.ps1` rereads `DFHACK_*` values through the same process-environment
  API used by `Import-EnvironmentFile`, so values newly loaded from `.env.local`
  configure compilation and live reload in that invocation.
- `Import-EnvironmentFile` now distinguishes an absent process variable from
  an explicitly empty one; the latter retains precedence over `.env.local`.
- `.env.local` is ignored by Git. No example file was added because the four
  supported `DFHACK_*` variables are explicit in the tool's parameters and
  error messages.

Focused disposable-fixture audit passed:

- no `DoThis` or `SoulSearch` identifier occurs in staged Build, syntax, or
  common tooling;
- an explicit `luac.exe` path and `DFHACK_LUAC` each syntax-checked a one-file
  source fixture; requiring Lua `0.0` failed with the expected version error;
- a one-file absolute `SourceDir` passed Build with `-LiveReload:$false` and
  reported one production plus 62 test/support files, proving the selected
  source directory was used instead of hard-coded `src`;
- `.env` comments, `export`, single/double quotes, missing optional files,
  invalid entries, and nonempty/empty process-variable precedence behaved as
  specified;
- `Resolve-DFHackRunner` resolved an explicit runner, a
  `<DwarfFortressRoot>/hack/dfhack-run.exe` fixture, and a PATH fixture; missing
  explicit paths and roots produced clear errors;
- default `LiveReload=$true` invoked `soulsearch reload`, printed mock runner
  output, wrote `ReloadOutputPath`, and on a mock exit code of 7 retained the
  captured output before throwing an exit-code error.

The staged build also passed against the real checkout with
`-LiveReload:$false`: Lua 5.4.6 syntax-checked 66 production and 62
test/support Lua files.

## Phase 2 - Establish the repo-local LuaUnit runner contract

☑ Add `.luarocks/` to `.gitignore` and confirm it cannot enter `src/` or a
  published package.
☑ Keep LuaUnit pinned to `3.5-1`; check and install that exact version into
  `.luarocks/` rather than accepting an arbitrary globally installed version.
☑ Replace `DOTHIS_TEST_FILES` with a documented project-neutral mechanism
  for passing the discovered suite list to `Tests/run.lua`.
☑ Make `Tests/run.lua` derive `repo_root`, `Tests/`, and
  `src/scripts_modinstalled/` paths from its own location so LuaUnit CLI
  arguments remain untouched.
☑ Preserve the caller's existing `LUA_PATH` while adding the repo-local rock
  path, test support path, and production-module path; restore process
  environment variables on both success and failure.
☑ Preserve passthrough of LuaUnit CLI arguments for targeted tests, output
  modes, verbosity, and failure controls.
☑ Ensure test discovery is deterministic, selects all current suite files,
  and excludes `run.lua`, support modules, and fixtures.
☑ Avoid terminating a parent PowerShell host unexpectedly when
  `Run-UnitTests.ps1` is invoked from another tool; expose a reliable nonzero
  process result without bypassing cleanup.
☑ Add one LuaUnit smoke suite that proves dependency loading, repository-root
  resolution, discovery, and a deliberate assertion failure path.

Exit gate: staged `NewTools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` can bootstrap pinned LuaUnit and
execute a discovered smoke suite with correct paths, arguments, cleanup, and
exit status without installing it into `tools/` yet.

Phase 2 recorded evidence: `.luarocks/` is ignored and outside `src/`, so it
cannot enter the package source tree. The runner requires LuaRocks, checks the
exact `luaunit 3.5-1` rock in that local tree, and installs it there when absent.
It supplies the sorted, newline-delimited `DFHACK_LUA_TEST_FILES` list and
appends repo-local rock, test, production, and caller paths to `LUA_PATH`; both
environment values are restored in `finally`.

On 2026-07-17, `luarocks install luaunit 3.5-1 --tree ./.luarocks` successfully
installed the pinned rock and `luarocks show` confirmed its local module path.
The earlier manifest error was caused by the restricted execution environment;
the same command succeeds with normal system/network access. The staged runner
then passed all three smoke tests and one targeted test. The deterministic
discovery scan found 56 current `*_test.lua`/`test_*.lua` files and no support,
fixture, or `run.lua` matches.

## Phase 3 - Bridge the existing test suite onto LuaUnit

☑ Introduce the smallest temporary compatibility adapter needed to map the
  current `test.case` and assertion API onto LuaUnit without changing test
  meaning.
☑ Preserve the existing per-file registration boundary while the adapter is
  active so every current suite continues to receive a valid repository root
  and isolated harness state.
☑ Map every custom assertion with equivalent failure semantics and useful
  messages:
  - true, false, and nil;
  - exact equality;
  - numeric tolerance;
  - ordered sequence equality.
☑ Make dynamic case names visible and uniquely identifiable in LuaUnit
  output.
☑ Execute all existing `*_test.lua` suites through the staged
  `NewTools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` runner and compare discovered file, case, and
  assertion totals with the Phase 0 baseline.
☑ Deliberately fail one representative domain assertion and one harness/UI
  assertion to prove failures are neither swallowed nor reported as passes;
  revert only the deliberate failures after recording the proof.

Exit gate: the complete pre-existing suite runs under LuaUnit with no omitted
suites or weakened assertions, while production code remains unchanged.

Phase 3 recorded evidence: `Tests/support/luaunit_compat.lua` creates one fresh
compatibility API per discovered legacy suite file. Each `test.case()` becomes
a uniquely named LuaUnit function in the form
`test <suite module> :: <dynamic case name>`, while each suite registration
still receives its own adapter and the resolved repository root. The adapter
preserves the legacy truthy/falsey, nil, exact `==`, numeric-tolerance, and
ordered-sequence semantics while reporting failures through LuaUnit.

On 2026-07-17 the staged runner discovered 56 test files: the 55 pre-existing
legacy suite files plus the Phase 2 LuaUnit smoke file. The full run passed 328
tests with zero failures: all 325 Phase 0 legacy cases plus three smoke cases.
The compatibility bridge exposed a new forward baseline of 2,001 executed
legacy assertion calls: true=535, false=169, nil=86, equal=1,056, near=8, and
sequence=147. Phase 0 exposed no assertion total, so file and case totals match
that baseline exactly while 2,001 becomes the first comparable assertion
baseline. A verbose targeted run displayed and executed exactly
`test attributes_test :: attributes: physical above race median`.

Two temporary negative probes were applied and reverted. The domain probe
reported `test attributes_test :: traits: caste-aware baseline and tier
distance` as failed with `expected 61, got 60`. The harness probe reported
`test script_harness_test :: script harness: supplies only explicit
dependencies and overrides` as failed with `expected 10, got 9`. Both produced
exit code 1 through `NewTools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`; the post-revert full run passed
again, and `git diff` confirmed neither probe nor any production `src/` change
remained.

## Phase 4 - Convert suites to native LuaUnit and retire the custom framework

☑ Convert domain/catalog/formatting suites to native LuaUnit structures and
  assertions; run the full suite after the batch.
☑ Convert candidate-provider, filter-state, preset, settings, search, and
  lifecycle suites; run the full suite after the batch.
☑ Convert module-loader, script-harness, DFHack-stub, and environment-heavy
  suites while preserving per-test isolation and global restoration; run the
  full suite after the batch.
☑ Convert UI component, overlay, pointer, tooltip, geometry, refresh, and
  characterization suites; run the full suite after the batch.
☑ Replace the explicit suite registry in `Tests/run.lua` with the proven
  deterministic discovery contract.
☑ Remove the temporary compatibility adapter and
  `Tests/support/testlib.lua` only after no suite requires them.
☑ Search the complete `Tests/` tree for old `test.case`, custom assertion,
  suite-registration, and positional `repo_root` conventions; the result must
  be empty except for intentional migration-history text in this plan.
☑ Compare final suite/case/assertion coverage with the Phase 0 baseline and
  explain any intentional count change in the execution evidence.

Exit gate: every SoulSearch test is natively executed and reported by LuaUnit,
the custom framework is gone, and the full suite retains baseline behavior and
coverage.

Phase 4 recorded evidence (2026-07-18): all 55 pre-existing suite files now
return native LuaUnit test tables and use LuaUnit assertions directly. The four
required batch boundaries each passed the complete 328-test run: the native
case count advanced from 3 smoke / 325 legacy to 68 / 260, 159 / 169,
208 / 120, and finally 328 / 0. After the last batch, `Tests/run.lua` was
simplified to load every sorted discovered test file uniformly and publish its
returned table under a deterministic `Test_<module>` name.

The final default run discovered 56 native files and passed 328 tests with zero
failures: the Phase 0 total of 55 suites and 325 cases is unchanged, and the
three intentional Phase 2 smoke cases explain the only count increase. A
temporary measurement run executed exactly 2,001 migrated assertion calls,
matching the Phase 3 forward baseline exactly; all 147 runtime sequence checks
used dense primitive sequences, confirming native ordered table equality has
the same observed comparison domain as the legacy shallow sequence assertion.
The source tree retains all 1,497 migrated assertion call sites plus five smoke
assertions (1,502 static calls total); runtime totals are higher because
table-driven cases execute some call sites repeatedly. Phase 0 itself exposed
no assertion count, so there is no Phase 0 assertion-total change to explain.

`Tests/support/luaunit_compat.lua` and `Tests/support/testlib.lua` were deleted
only after a mixed-mode full run reported 56 native suites and zero legacy
suites. A complete `Tests/` scan then returned no old `test.case`, custom
assertion, registration-function, custom-framework require, or positional
repository-root convention matches. Repository-root resolution now lives in
the ordinary path helper `Tests/support/repo_root.lua`, not a test framework.
Final validation passed the default 328-test run, a one-case verbose target,
and the reusable build's Lua 5.4.6 syntax check of 66 production plus 63
test/support files. The native smoke failure opt-in produced exactly one visible
failure and propagated exit code 1 through `Run-UnitTests.ps1`; the environment
override was removed immediately afterward.

## Phase 5 - Migrate publishing and package verification

☑ Audit `NewTools/Publish.ps1` and `VerifyPackage.ps1` for sibling-project
  leakage and confirm all project identity comes from `src/info.txt` or
  explicit parameters.
☑ Reconcile `Publish.ps1 -SourceDir` with its build invocation so a custom
  source selection is consistently validated, packaged, and verified.
☑ Preserve the new publish behavior that calls the build with
  `-LiveReload:$false`; publishing must not require or trigger a running game.
☑ Record and adopt one intentional zip-root contract, then make
  `Publish.ps1`, `VerifyPackage.ps1`, README installation instructions, and
  tests agree on it.
☑ Record whether the removed `NoRootFolder` and `NoExpandedFolder` options
  have any remaining callers; either preserve a generic equivalent or update
  every caller and document their removal.
☑ Preserve byte-for-byte/SHA-256 manifest comparison between `src/`, the
  expanded package, and the extracted zip, with useful missing, unexpected,
  and differing-file diagnostics.
☑ Move SoulSearch-specific bootstrap checks out of the generic verifier and
  into focused project tests so generic verification does not weaken these
  invariants:
  - required `soulsearch.lua` annotations;
  - keybinding bootstrap dependency;
  - public command and required metadata presence.
☑ Verify both repository-relative and absolute source/package paths.
☑ Verify temporary staging/extraction directories are cleaned after success
  and after forced verification failure.
☑ Verify tests, tools, `.luarocks/`, `.env.local`, docs, and planning files
  are absent from both package forms.

Exit gate: publishing is reusable and non-live, both package forms exactly
match the intended source payload, and SoulSearch-specific package guarantees
remain covered by project tests.

Phase 5 recorded evidence (2026-07-18): `Publish.ps1` and
`VerifyPackage.ps1` contain no SoulSearch, DoThis, DwarfSearch, or legacy
project-environment identifiers. Package name and displayed version are
required from the selected source's `info.txt`, with only the package name
allowing an explicit override. The fixture contract published once as the
metadata name `FixtureMod` and once as the explicit name `ExplicitPackage`.

`Publish.ps1` now passes its resolved `SourceDir` to `Build.ps1` together with
`-LiveReload:$false`, and passes the same path to staging and verification. A
fixture with invalid Lua failed during the custom-source build, proving the
selected source is validated; successful fixture publishes with deliberately
invalid DFHack runner/root environment values proved no live runner is
resolved or invoked.

The adopted archive contract is flat root: `info.txt` and
`scripts_modinstalled/` are archive-root entries, while the expanded package
is `<OutputDir>/<metadata-or-explicit-name>/`. Publish uses .NET directory
archiving without a wrapper, VerifyPackage compares the extraction root
directly, README installation text gives the matching extraction procedure,
and `Tests/package_tools_test.ps1` rejects a wrapper-root archive. Repository
search found no live callers of the removed `NoRootFolder` or
`NoExpandedFolder` switches; both were intentionally removed because Phase 5
requires both fixed-shape package forms on every publish and verification.

The generic verifier now reports complete missing, unexpected, or differing
path lists and compares SHA-256 manifests for source, expanded output, and the
extracted zip. Fixture probes independently produced and recognized
`missing: payload.bin`, `unexpected: unexpected.txt`, and
`differing: payload.bin`; an unexpected zip entry and wrapper-root zip also
failed. Absolute paths passed, and repository-relative paths passed while the
caller was in a different working directory.

SoulSearch package invariants now belong to the discovered native LuaUnit
suite `Tests/package_contract_test.lua`, which requires nonempty ID, name,
numeric/displayed version, and description metadata; both public command
files; the `--@module=true` and `--@enable=true` annotations; the bootstrap's
keybinding reference; and the keybinding module itself. The full suite passed
331 tests (the Phase 4 total plus three intentional package-contract cases),
and the generic verifier retains only project-neutral DFHack payload shape and
manifest checks.

The fixture test observed no new `DFHackModPublish-*` or
`DFHackModVerify-*` directories after successful publish/verification, after a
verifier stub forced publisher verification failure, after a locked payload
forced staging-copy failure, or after a tampered zip forced extraction
verification failure; its unique workspace fixture was also removed. The real
publisher then created and verified `dist/SoulSearch-0.1.0.zip` plus
`dist/SoulSearch/`: all 67 source files matched both forms, the zip had
`info.txt` at its root, and scans found zero tests, tools, `NewTools`,
`.luarocks`, `.env.local`, docs, or `.todo` files in either package.

## Phase 6 - Perform the atomic toolchain cutover

